# The Six Orthodox Schools: Systems of Vedic Philosophy

> Six darshanas — "viewpoints" — accepted the Vedas and divided the
> intellectual labour: logic, atomism, dualism, meditation, ritual
> interpretation, and ultimate reality.

## 1. What "Orthodox" Means

A **darshana** is a system, literally a "view". All six orthodox
schools are **astika**: each accepts the Vedas as a pramana, even
where — like Samkhya and early Mimamsa — it has no creator God.
Tradition pairs each theory school with an applied partner:

@diagram tree id="philosophy-orthodox-pairs" layout="tree" aria="The six orthodox schools arranged in three pairs"
  "Six orthodox schools" -> "Nyaya with Vaisheshika"
  "Six orthodox schools" -> "Samkhya with Yoga"
  "Six orthodox schools" -> "Mimamsa with Vedanta"
@/diagram

## 2. Nyaya: The Science of Logic

Founded by **Gautama (Akshapada)** in the **Nyayasutra**, Nyaya is the
school of logic and debate. It admits **four pramanas**: perception
(pratyaksha), inference (anumana), comparison (upamana), and testimony
(shabda). Inference runs five steps — proposition, reason, example,
application, conclusion — and Nyaya catalogued the pseudo-reasons
(hetvabhasa).

[definition]
**Pramana** means a valid source of knowledge. Schools are often
distinguished simply by how many pramanas they admit — Charvaka one,
Buddhists two, Nyaya four, Vedanta up to six.
[/definition]

Later Naiyayikas such as Udayana used inference to argue for God as
the intelligent cause of the world, making Nyaya the great debating
engine against Buddhist logicians.

## 3. Vaisheshika: Atoms and Categories

**Kanada's** **Vaisheshikasutra** builds a naturalist physics. Its
famous **seven padarthas (categories)** cover everything knowable:

| Category | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Substance (dravya) | Earth, water, fire, air, ether, time, space, self, mind |
| Quality (guna) | Colour, taste, touch |
| Action (karma) | Rising, falling, moving |
| Generality (samanya) | "Cowness" in every cow |
| Particularity (vishesha) | What individuates atoms |
| Inherence (samavaya) | The tie between a whole and its parts |
| Non-existence (abhava) | Absence, as of a pot here |

Its **atomism** holds that irreducible atoms (paramanu) of earth,
water, fire, and air combine into compounds, and that the world
undergoes cycles of creation and dissolution — later read, together
with Nyaya, as guided by God.

## 4. Samkhya: The Two Realities

The oldest systematised school, attributed to **Kapila**, is a
dualism of two ultimate realities:

- **Purusha** — consciousness: passive, countless, witness-only.
- **Prakriti** — primordial matter: one, active, unconscious.

Prakriti's display is woven from **three gunas**: **sattva**
(lucidity), **rajas** (energy), and **tamas** (inertia). When its
equilibrium disturbs, it unfolds into **25 tattvas**:

| Tattvas | Layer |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Prakriti — primal nature |
| 2–4 | Intellect (mahat), ego-maker, mind |
| 5–14 | Ten senses, cognitive and motor |
| 15–19 | Five subtle elements (tanmatras) |
| 20–24 | Five gross elements (mahabhutas) |
| 25 | Purusha — the witnessing self |

Liberation (**kaivalya**) is the discriminating realisation that "I am
not this display" — purusha stands apart, and prakriti, unobserved,
retires.

## 5. Yoga: Stilling the Mind

**Patanjali's** **Yoga Sutras** (c. 2nd century BCE, dating debated)
accept Samkhya's metaphysics but add Ishvara — a special, untouched
purusha. The opening definition is the whole programme: *yogash
chitta-vritti-nirodhah* — **yoga is the cessation of the fluctuations
of the mind**.

| Limb | Name | Practise |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Yama | Restraints: non-violence, truth, non-stealing |
| 2 | Niyama | Observances: purity, study, devotion |
| 3 | Asana | Steady posture |
| 4 | Pranayama | Breath regulation |
| 5 | Pratyahara | Withdrawal of the senses |
| 6 | Dharana | Fixed attention |
| 7 | Dhyana | Sustained meditation |
| 8 | Samadhi | Absorbed union |

## 6. Mimamsa: The Ritual Science of Words

**Jaimini's** **Mimamsasutra** (Purva Mimamsa — "the earlier inquiry")
is the hermeneutics of Vedic ritual. Its revolutionary claims:

- **Apaurusheyatva** — the Vedas are not the work of any author,
  human or divine, and so cannot be false.
- **Svatah pramanya** — Vedic statements are valid in themselves,
  needing no external confirmation, unlike perception-based knowledge.
- Dharma is what the Vedic injunction commands; action's results need
  no God to distribute them (early Mimamsa is functionally atheist).

## 7. Vedanta: The Science of Brahman

**Badarayana's** **Brahmasutras** (also called Uttara Mimamsa — "the
later inquiry") systematise the Upanishads into about 555 short sutras
in four chapters, and became the seedbed of Shankara, Ramanuja, and
Madhva — the subject of the next lesson.

## 8. The Systems Side by Side

[note]
The six schools are allies only at the level of Vedic loyalty. Samkhya
argued with Yoga over Ishvara, Nyaya with Vedanta over God's nature,
and everyone with the Buddhists — debate culture (vada) itself became
India's shared scientific method.
[/note]

| School | Founder | Root text | Core claim | Reality count |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Nyaya | Gautama (Akshapada) | Nyayasutra | Logic; four pramanas | 9 substances |
| Vaisheshika | Kanada | Vaisheshikasutra | Atomism; 7 categories | 9 substances |
| Samkhya | Kapila | Samkhyakarika | Purusha–prakriti dualism | 25 tattvas |
| Yoga | Patanjali | Yoga Sutras | Eight limbs still the mind | 25 tattvas + Ishvara |
| Mimamsa | Jaimini | Mimamsasutra | Vedas authorless and self-valid | Endless ritual order |
| Vedanta | Badarayana | Brahmasutras | Brahman is the ultimate | 1 — Brahman |
